issue_context.body:
I have run into an issue I think might be a bug but I am not sure at the moment.

The problem can be produced by writing a string using shell_fprintf().  This occurs when the shell is registered as a backend for the logging system.  Here is an excerpt from main.c:
```
	while (log_process(false) == true) {
	}
	/* a delay is required here to flush the log buffer */
	/* otherwise, the shell is unresponsive after */
	/* the banner is printed */
	k_sleep(K_SECONDS(2));	
	const shell *sh = shell_backend_uart_get_ptr();
	shell_fprintf(sh, SHELL_VT100_COLOR_BLUE, "%s", STRING_SCAN_LOGO);
	shell_fprintf(sh, SHELL_VT100_COLOR_BLUE, "%s", STRING_WITAP_LOGO);
```
The strings are Figlet art strings with about 443 characters and 207 characters respectively.  I replaced the strings with a single shell_fprintf() and a short string "This is a short string" and the same issue occurs.  So, I do not believe this is related to the size of the string.

I am using the usb CDC_ACM for the shell console:
```
CONFIG_UART_SHELL_ON_DEV_NAME="CDC_ACM_0"
```
I have a short loop in main.c I use to wait for the DTR after boot before the printf:
```
		u8_t i = 0;
		u32_t dtr = 0U;

		while (true) {
			uart_line_ctrl_get(dev, UART_LINE_CTRL_DTR, &dtr);
			if (dtr) {
				LOG_DBG("Terminal ready");
				break;
			} else {
				if ( k_timer_status_get(&loop_timeout_timer) > 0 ) {
					// if we time out, detach the USB
					usb_disable();
					break;
				}
				if ((i++ % 30) == 0) {
					wdt_feed(wdt, wdt_channel_id);
					led_add(&animation_wait);
				}
				k_sleep(K_MSEC(100));
			}
		}
```
I am running a screen session as my VT100 terminal:
```
screen /dev/cu.usbmodem4013301
```
I have noticed that if there is delay between the DTR signal when I start the screen session and when I press the first keyboard character, the Figlet art appears in the screen session, a shell prompt is displayed but the shell becomes unresponsive.  I believe the main application thread is still running because I can see events occur (led heartbeats that fire on k_timer events).

If I press a key immediately after the DTR when I start the screen session, a few buffered log messages appear in the screen session, the Figlet art appears, a shell prompt appears and the shell is responsive.

I think the issue is related to some conflict or deadlock between the buffered log messages and the shell.  I tried flushing log messages using log_process() as shown above and inserting some delay but the problem still occurs.

I am not an expert on the shell system so I have not been able to determine exactly where the problem occurs and suggest a fix.

comments:
I have determined that the underlying reason that the shell is unresponsive is because the shell thread does not appear to be running properly.  The thread never reaches the while loop shown below when the problem occurs.

shell.c:
```
	/* Enable shell and print prompt. */
	err = shell_start(shell);
	if (err != 0) {
		return;
	}

	while (true) {
		/* waiting for all signals except SHELL_SIGNAL_TXDONE */
		err = k_poll(shell->ctx->events, SHELL_SIGNAL_TXDONE,
			     K_FOREVER);

		k_mutex_lock(&shell->ctx->wr_mtx, K_FOREVER);
```
The deadlock appears to occur at the k_poll() in shell_pend_on_txdone() shown below.  The thread never reaches the k_poll_signal_reset().
```
static void shell_pend_on_txdone(const struct shell *shell)
{
	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_MULTITHREADING) &&
	    (shell->ctx->state < SHELL_STATE_PANIC_MODE_ACTIVE)) {
		k_poll(&shell->ctx->events[SHELL_SIGNAL_TXDONE], 1, K_FOREVER);
		k_poll_signal_reset(&shell->ctx->signals[SHELL_SIGNAL_TXDONE]);
	} else {
		/* Blocking wait in case of bare metal. */
```
I have confirmed transport_evt_handler() receives the SHELL_TRANSPORT_EVT_TX_RDY event which then raises the SHELL_SIGNAL_TXDONE signal.  But, for some reason, the signal is not received by shell_pend_on_txdone() and therefore the function does not unblock.
```
static void transport_evt_handler(enum shell_transport_evt evt_type, void *ctx)
{
	struct shell *shell = (struct shell *)ctx;
	struct k_poll_signal *signal;

	signal = (evt_type == SHELL_TRANSPORT_EVT_RX_RDY) ?
			&shell->ctx->signals[SHELL_SIGNAL_RXRDY] :
			&shell->ctx->signals[SHELL_SIGNAL_TXDONE];
	k_poll_signal_raise(signal, 0);
}
```

The problem is that shell_start() prints some data to the output.  I fixed the problem by using the wr_mtx mutex around the output in shell_start().
```
	k_mutex_lock(&shell->ctx->wr_mtx, K_FOREVER);

	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SHELL_VT100_COLORS)) {
		shell_vt100_color_set(shell, SHELL_NORMAL);
	}

	shell_raw_fprintf(shell->fprintf_ctx, "\n\n");

	state_set(shell, SHELL_STATE_ACTIVE);

	k_mutex_unlock(&shell->ctx->wr_mtx);
```
This creates another problem though.  The shell now blocks while waiting for a keypress, the watchdog will timeout if the keypress does not occur before the wdt timeout.

Does anyone have a suggestion about how to disable the shell waiting for an initial keypress?

