Did you intend your comment to mean calling shell_fprintf() from anything but the shell context as shown in the example above is forbidden?

I compiled an experiment where I put a lock here:
```
void shell_fprintf_fmt(const struct shell_fprintf *sh_fprintf,
		       const char *fmt, va_list args)
{
	k_mutex_lock(&sh_fprintf->ctrl_blk->print_mtx, K_FOREVER);
#if !defined(CONFIG_NEWLIB_LIBC) && !defined(CONFIG_ARCH_POSIX)
	(void)z_prf(out_func, (void *)sh_fprintf, (char *)fmt, args);
#else
	z_vprintk(out_func, (void *)sh_fprintf, fmt, args);
#endif

	if (sh_fprintf->ctrl_blk->autoflush) {
		shell_fprintf_buffer_flush(sh_fprintf);
	}
	k_mutex_unlock(&sh_fprintf->ctrl_blk->print_mtx);
}
```
I declare the lock in the shell_fprintf_control_block struct and it is initialized in instance_init().

I am wondering what situations would arise where this would be a problem?

@sslupsky : I've started working on that, sorry for the delay.
Basically, the shell allows using only functions/macros that are protected by the mutex like `shell_fprintf`. It is calling `shell_vfprintf` that has the mutex.

Function `shell_fprintf_fmt` can be called internally by the shell because it assumes that the mutex is taken by the shell thread.  This function does not belong to shell API and shall not be used. You cannot add mutex there.
